WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.439 --> 00:00:08.069
Fans have a close relationship with their
favorite shows. But sometimes, those shows

00:00:08.069 --> 00:00:13.019
let us down, and they actually have to apologize
for doing us wrong. Here are a few that had

00:00:13.019 --> 00:00:15.740
to say they were sorry to mend our broken
little hearts.

00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:17.830
Chuck Lorre for Two and a Half Men

00:00:17.830 --> 00:00:22.080
Two and a Half Men had enough of an audience
to last for 12 seasons, but no one ever made

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:27.080
the mistake of confusing it with quality TV.
Not even the show's co-creator Chuck Lorre.

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:32.550
At the 2014 Television Academy Honors, Lorre
won an award for his latest show, Mom, and

00:00:32.550 --> 00:00:35.829
used his speech to apologize for bringing
it into the world at all:

00:00:35.829 --> 00:00:42.809
"To do a show about people trying to redeem
their lives and to salvage and repair the

00:00:42.809 --> 00:00:51.390
damage they've done, well, for me that's an
opportunity to apologize for Two and a Half

00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:53.409
Men."

00:00:55.540 --> 00:00:59.499
Hmmm. Apology...not accepted.

00:00:59.499 --> 00:01:01.170
Dan Harmon for Community

00:01:01.170 --> 00:01:05.350
The cult sitcom Community seemed to be constantly
embroiled in trouble during its six-season

00:01:05.350 --> 00:01:10.110
run. In an effort to alleviate some of the
tension, NBC fired creator and showrunner

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:11.310
Dan Harmon.

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:18.100
"Buenos dios, children!"

00:01:18.110 --> 00:01:22.970
After fans protested and ratings dropped,
the network brought Harmon back. In celebration,

00:01:22.970 --> 00:01:27.410
the embittered Harmon publicly trashed the
episodes filmed without him. Not cool.

00:01:35.930 --> 00:01:40.700
Harmon quickly wrote a very long apology directed
towards the show's fans, actors, and crew,

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:45.360
saying, "Even if my goal had been to hurt
someone, it would never have been you."

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:47.090
Chris Evans for Top Gear

00:01:47.090 --> 00:01:51.840
When Top Gear lost all three of its popular
hosts after Jeremy Clarkson's infamous punch-drunk

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:56.210
assault of the show's crew, the BBC scrambled
to keep its top-rated show running with new

00:01:56.210 --> 00:02:01.000
hosts and new gimmicks. Unfortunately, one
of those gimmicks included doing donuts around

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:05.840
a respected war memorial, Whitehall's Cenotaph.
The stunt was quickly called out by veterans

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:10.009
before the show even got to air. New host
Chris Evans apologized, saying he and the

00:02:10.009 --> 00:02:13.670
crew were "mortified," and promised that shots
of the memorial would be cut out before the

00:02:13.670 --> 00:02:16.370
show made it to air. Good idea.

00:02:16.370 --> 00:02:19.640
Everyone on Star Trek: The Next Generation
for “Code of Honor”

00:02:19.640 --> 00:02:23.310
Watching the third episode of Star Trek: The
Next Generation is enough to leave you astonished

00:02:23.310 --> 00:02:27.250
that the show actually made it through a single
season. "Code of Honor" portrays a backwards

00:02:27.250 --> 00:02:31.420
vision of a tribal space-Africa. While the
episode attempts to deliver a message about

00:02:31.420 --> 00:02:36.819
accepting different cultures, it misses spectacularly,
falling into blatant racism and sexism.

00:02:36.819 --> 00:02:41.490
"Yar is to become my First One."
"I challenge your right of supersedence!"

00:02:41.490 --> 00:02:44.280
"No woman has challenged supersedence for
over two hundred years!"

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:46.569
"The right is mine, and I will have it."

00:02:46.569 --> 00:02:51.280
At different times, almost everyone involved
with the show has apologized for its existence.

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:55.380
Actor Jonathan Frakes stated that Trek creator
Gene Roddenberry would have been embarrassed.

00:02:55.380 --> 00:02:59.750
Actor Brent Spiner remarked that the show
never got "quite that bad again," and Michael

00:02:59.750 --> 00:03:03.569
Dorn simply called it "the worst ever filmed."

00:03:03.569 --> 00:03:06.660
Robert Kirkman for The Walking Dead season
6 finale

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:11.180
For The Walking Dead comic fans, Negan is
the ultimate villain, and the entire TV series

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:15.990
was a buildup to the moment that his barbed-wire
bat came down on a beloved cast member's dome—a

00:03:15.990 --> 00:03:19.510
moment they were cheated out of when the show
cut to black at the end of season 6:

00:03:19.510 --> 00:03:30.630
"You can breath, you can blink, you can cry.
Hell, you're all gonna be doing that."

00:03:30.630 --> 00:03:34.970
Fans were enraged at the cliffhanger, prompting
creator Robert Kirkman to apologize in the

00:03:34.970 --> 00:03:38.750
letters page of The Walking Dead #154. Sort
of.

00:03:38.750 --> 00:03:43.739
"For some of you, [the cliffhanger] backfired
and you're angry. And for that I'm sorry."

00:03:43.739 --> 00:03:48.080
It’s not much of an apology, but it's better
than nothing. Thanks?

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:51.569
And thank you for watching! Subscribe to our
YouTube channel to watch more videos like

00:03:51.569 --> 00:03:55.050
the one you just saw. And leave us a comment
to let us know which TV shows you think owe

00:03:55.050 --> 00:03:56.099
us all an apology...

